            <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/2096116/pexels-photo-2096116.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1200" alt="Water dripping from a ceiling, showing a leak inside the home" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <span class="w2-service-photo-label">LEAK DETECTION</span>
            <figcaption class="w2-service-photo-caption">Ceiling leak <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/grayscale-photo-of-water-dropping-from-ceiling-2096116/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Photo: Pexels</a></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="w2-service-detail">
            <p class="w2-service-overline">02 <span>Interior plumbing</span></p>
            <h3 id="w2-service-title-wall">Wall &amp; ceiling leak detection</h3>
            <p class="w2-service-description">A hidden leak inside a wall or above a ceiling may first appear as a stain, peeling or bubbling paint, soft drywall, a musty odor, or a drip that returns after the area dries. Water can travel along framing, so the visible mark may not be directly below the source. A detection visit considers the affected rooms, nearby fixtures, plumbing routes, and when the dampness appears. The inspection follows those clues to identify the area most likely connected to a plumbing leak and distinguishes it from other possible sources when the evidence allows. Findings are explained clearly, including what was inspected and where moisture or leak indicators were found. Roof or exterior-wall water intrusion may require a different specialist.</p>
            <div class="w2-service-clues"><span>Ceiling stains</span><span>Damp drywall</span><span>Musty odor</span></div>
            <a class="w2-service-action" href="#contact">Request wall &amp; ceiling detection <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
