            <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/31314903/pexels-photo-31314903.jpeg?auto=compress&amp;cs=tinysrgb&amp;w=1200" alt="Worker in an excavation beside exposed underground pipes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <span class="w2-service-photo-label">LEAK DETECTION</span>
            <figcaption class="w2-service-photo-caption">Underground pipe inspection <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/man-working-in-muddy-excavation-site-with-pipes-31314903/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Photo: Pexels</a></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="w2-service-detail">
            <p class="w2-service-overline">03 <span>Water lines outdoors</span></p>
            <h3 id="w2-service-title-underground">Underground water line leak detection</h3>
            <p class="w2-service-description">A buried water line can leak beneath a lawn, driveway, walkway, or paved area without leaving an obvious puddle. Signs may include a strip of ground that stays wet in dry weather, unusually green growth, a pressure change, or meter movement when water should be off. Detection begins with the line route from the meter toward the building, surface conditions, and symptoms you have noticed. The technician uses those details to narrow the section that may be losing water and explain what the inspection indicates. Locating the suspected area before any excavation is considered can help you avoid searching blindly. You receive clear findings about the likely line and location to guide your own decision about what should happen next.</p>
            <div class="w2-service-clues"><span>Persistently wet ground</span><span>Pressure changes</span><span>Meter movement</span></div>
            <a class="w2-service-action" href="#contact">Request water line leak detection <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </article>

