            <span class="w2-service-photo-label">LEAK DETECTION</span>
            <figcaption class="w2-service-photo-caption">Indoor pipe inspection <a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/plumber-repairing-pipe-with-wrench-indoors-32588548/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Photo: Pexels</a></figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="w2-service-detail">
            <p class="w2-service-overline">04 <span>Inside the plumbing system</span></p>
            <h3 id="w2-service-title-plumbing">Interior plumbing leak detection</h3>
            <p class="w2-service-description">Not every hidden leak is beneath a slab or outdoors. Supply pipes, shutoff valves, connections, and fixture plumbing can seep inside cabinets, behind appliances, or in utility spaces. Clues include recurring moisture, swollen cabinet material, corrosion, a damp smell, or a meter that continues moving while fixtures are closed. The inspection checks the affected area and connected lines to assess whether the evidence points to a plumbing leak, condensation, a spill, or a fixture issue. The technician records the signs and explains the likely source and limits of the inspection. This service is focused on locating and documenting the leak; it does not include plumbing repairs. Clear findings can help you decide which qualified provider to contact for any work that may be needed afterward.</p>
            <div class="w2-service-clues"><span>Cabinet moisture</span><span>Meter movement</span><span>Visible corrosion</span></div>
            <a class="w2-service-action" href="#contact">Request plumbing leak detection <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
          </div>
        </article>

